{"alignments":[{"id":"alignment:8083d627495b82121e4e2432","layer":"US","nodeId":"math:complex-quadratic-solutions","reason":"Solves the intended nonreal-root case for arbitrary real quadratic coefficients, with nonzero leading coefficient, both conjugate roots, and a check in the original or equivalent equation. Complex-coefficient quadratics are not added to this standard's scope.","relation":"exact","reviewProvenance":{"evidenceFile":"us-gap-high.json","sourceCode":"N-CN.C.7","sourceReviewStatus":"mapped","type":"subsequent_content_scope_review"},"reviewStatus":"machine_content_review","sourceNodeId":"626","status":"accepted"}],"candidates":[{"additionalConceptCount":4,"alignmentIds":["alignment:8083d627495b82121e4e2432"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: 6","reason":"Set w = z+2i. The polynomial becomes w^3+w+10 = (w+2)(w^2-2w+5), with roots -2 and 1+/-2i. Thus the original roots are -2-2i, 1, and 1-4i. The last two form a vertical base of length 4, and the first has horizontal distance 3 from that base. The area is 4*3/2 = 6.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["math:complex-quadratic-solutions"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2025_AMC_12A_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h100"],"id":"center-shift-and-factor","label":"Translate the variable and factor","methodDirectNodeIds":{"center-shift-and-factor":["math:polynomial-remainder-factor","math:complex-quadratic-solutions","cnm_h098","cnm_p148"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h100","math:polynomial-remainder-factor","math:complex-quadratic-solutions","cnm_h098","cnm_p148"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"alignmentIds":["alignment:8083d627495b82121e4e2432"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A (7)","reason":"The roots give points (1,0), (-1,±sqrt(3)), and (-2,±sqrt(2)). Their ellipse is 5x^2+6y^2+9x-14=0, or 5(x+9/10)^2+6y^2=361/20. Thus a^2=361/100, b^2=361/120, and eccentricity squared is 1-b^2/a^2=1/6. Therefore m+n=7.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["math:complex-quadratic-solutions"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_AMC_12A_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"symmetric-ellipse-fitting","label":"Fit an ellipse using symmetry","methodDirectNodeIds":{"symmetric-ellipse-fitting":["math:complex-quadratic-solutions","cnm_h098","cnm_136","cnm_h184","cnm_090","cnm_h185"]},"nodeIds":["math:complex-quadratic-solutions","cnm_h098","cnm_136","cnm_h184","cnm_090","cnm_h185"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:8083d627495b82121e4e2432"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, sqrt(10)","reason":"The four fixed roots are 1±i and 2±2i. The middle quadratic's roots have product 4; the only eligible pairs are (1+i, 2-2i) and (1-i, 2+2i). Their sums give c=3±i, so |c|=sqrt(9+1)=sqrt(10).","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["math:complex-quadratic-solutions"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2019_AMC_12A_Q14","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"pair-existing-roots","label":"Pair roots using their product","methodDirectNodeIds":{"pair-existing-roots":["math:complex-quadratic-solutions","cnm_226","cnm_h102","cnm_h098"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_226","cnm_h102","math:complex-quadratic-solutions","cnm_h098"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"substitute-shared-root","label":"Substitute a shared root","methodDirectNodeIds":{"substitute-shared-root":["math:complex-quadratic-solutions","math:polynomial-remainder-factor","cnm_h103","cnm_h098"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-remainder-factor","cnm_h103","math:complex-quadratic-solutions","cnm_h098"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:8083d627495b82121e4e2432"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 7","reason":"Taking moduli gives |z|^5=|z|, so z=0 or |z|=1. In the latter case, conjugate(z)=1/z, giving z^6=1. Its six distinct roots all satisfy the original equation, for seven solutions total.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["math:complex-quadratic-solutions"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2023_AMC_12A_Q14","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"modulus-and-algebraic-roots","label":"Compare moduli, then factor","methodDirectNodeIds":{"modulus-and-algebraic-roots":["cnm_h098","cnm_h104","math:roots-of-unity","math:complex-quadratic-solutions"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h098","cnm_h104","math:roots-of-unity","math:complex-quadratic-solutions"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:8083d627495b82121e4e2432"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 21; maximum imaginary part is √19/2","reason":"Set w=z+1/2. Then |w^2+3/4|=4, so |w|^2=|w^2|≤4+3/4=19/4. Hence Im(z)≤√19/2. Equality occurs at w=i√19/2, where w^2+3/4=-4. Thus m+n=19+2=21.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["math:complex-quadratic-solutions"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2023_AMC_12A_Q16","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"factor-and-bound-distances","label":"Bound the two factor distances","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factor-and-bound-distances":["math:complex-quadratic-solutions","cnm_h098","cnm_h102","cnm_096"]},"nodeIds":["math:complex-quadratic-solutions","cnm_h098","cnm_h102","cnm_096"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"alignmentIds":["alignment:8083d627495b82121e4e2432"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 2√21","reason":"A={2,-1+i√3,-1-i√3}, while the second polynomial factors as (z-8)(z²-8), giving B={8,2√2,-2√2}. For b∈B, squared distances are (b-2)² or (b+1)²+3. Their maximum is 84, attained between 8 and either nonreal point of A, so the greatest distance is 2√21.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["math:complex-quadratic-solutions"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2020_AMC_12A_Q15","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"factor-and-compare-squared-distances","label":"Factor and compare distances","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factor-and-compare-squared-distances":["math:sum-difference-cubes","math:complex-quadratic-solutions","cnm_151","cnm_h098","cnm_h173"]},"nodeIds":["math:sum-difference-cubes","math:complex-quadratic-solutions","cnm_151","cnm_h098","cnm_h173"]}]}],"canonicalNodeIds":["math:complex-quadratic-solutions"],"label":"N-CN.C.7","layer":"US","meaning":"Practice covers aligned canonical concepts, with no claim of complete standard coverage or mastery.","nextOffset":null,"sourceNode":{"code":"N-CN.C.7","description":"Solve quadratic equations with real coefficients that have complex solutions.","id":"626","label":"N-CN.C.7","metadata":{"clusterId":"147","clusterName":"Use Complex Numbers In Polynomial Identities And Equations.","clusterOrdinal":"C","domainCode":"N-CN","domainId":"117","domainName":"The Complex Number System","ordinal":"7","sourceCode":"HS.N-CN.C.7"},"parentSourceId":null,"placement":{"grade":"HS","meaning":"High school band, not an exact year"},"reviewStatus":"source_only"},"sourceNodeId":"626","status":"mapped","total":6}
